  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Serco obligated $992M in FY2026 to date across 437 awards, with an average award size of $2.3M. Serco obligations were down 23.6% year over year. Serco obligations by fiscal year were $773M in FY21, $873M in FY22, $1.1B in FY23, $1.5B in FY24, $1.3B in FY25, and $992M in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $652M so far in FY2026. The Department of Health and Human Services is the second-largest buyer, obligating $202M. Engineering Services is the largest NAICS market for Serco in FY2026 to date, accounting for $376M. Computer Systems Design Services and Security Systems Services (except Locksmiths) are the next-largest markets, accounting for $197M and $101M respectively. The note on the page clarifies that all figures are contract obligations except award-value figures in the largest-awards and expiring-contracts tables, which are total contract values, and that current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date). This snapshot summarizes who is buying from Serco and which markets are driving the company’s federal obligations in FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Serco's top agency buyers in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $652M from Serco in FY2026 to date. The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $202M. Other top buyers include the Pension Benefit Guaranty Corporation with $40.7M and the Department of Transportation with $33.7M. Remaining top-ten agencies range from $31.2M down to $2.5M in obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which markets drive Serco's federal revenue in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Engineering Services accounted for $376M of Serco obligations in FY2026 to date. Computer Systems Design Services accounted for $197M and Security Systems Services (except Locksmiths) accounted for $101M. Additional notable markets include Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $79.3M and Other Communications Equipment Manufacturing at $38.1M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Serco make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "Serco obligated $992M from federal contracts in FY2026 to date. Serco received that total across 437 awards with an average award size of $2.3M, and obligations were down 23.6% year over year."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from Serco?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense is Serco's largest buyer at $652M in FY2026 to date, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services at $202M. Other agencies in the top ten include the Pension Benefit Guaranty Corporation at $40.7M and the Department of Transportation at $33.7M, with additional agencies ranging from $31.2M to $2.5M."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Serco sell to the government?",
      "a": "Serco sells services in engineering and IT-heavy markets, led by Engineering Services at $376M and Computer Systems Design Services at $197M. Serco also sells Security Systems Services (except Locksmiths) at $101M and provides consulting and manufacturing-related services such as Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $79.3M."
    },
